---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Lossless by Design"
  description="Ethernet drops under congestion and lets a higher layer recover. A collective finishes when its slowest member finishes, so it cannot afford that — InfiniBand buys credit before it sends."
  heading="Lossless by Design"
  slug="/lossless"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      The network that joins these racks makes one promise the internet never made: it will not
      drop your packet. Everything else in Part II — the routing, the topology, the switch
      silicon — is downstream of that promise and of what it costs to keep.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why">Why dropping is unaffordable here</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Ordinary networks are allowed to throw packets away. If a switch runs out of room, it
        discards what it cannot hold, and software at each end eventually notices something is
        missing and asks again. That works well when the traffic is thousands of unrelated
        conversations, because only the unlucky conversation waits.
      </p>
      <p>
        The traffic here is not unrelated. When 9,216 GPUs combine their results, the operation is
        not finished until the <em>last</em> one has contributed. So a single packet thrown away
        does not delay one conversation — it delays every GPU in the job, all of which sit idle
        until the missing piece is asked for again and arrives.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why this network is built so the situation cannot arise. Instead of sending and
        hoping, a sender is told in advance exactly how much room the receiver has, and is not
        permitted to exceed it.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Ethernet was designed to drop. A switch with a full buffer discards the frame, and TCP
        infers the loss from missing acknowledgements and retransmits. The recovery is measured in
        round trips at best and in retransmission timeouts at worst, and the design is sound
        because it assumes statistically independent flows: the cost is paid by one flow while the
        rest keep moving.
      </p>
      <p>
        A collective breaks that assumption completely. An all-reduce is a synchronising barrier —
        its completion time is the completion time of its slowest participant — so loss anywhere
        stalls everywhere. Worse, the traffic is maximally correlated: every GPU transmits at
        the same instant on the same layer boundary, which is precisely the pattern that fills
        buffers.
      </p>
      <p>
        InfiniBand removes the failure mode rather than the recovery path.
        <T id="credit-flow-control">Credit-based flow control</T> has the receiver advertise how
        much buffer space it has, and the sender may not exceed it. Congestion therefore becomes a
        stall at the sender, never a discard in the fabric.<Cite ids="ieee-ib-credits" />
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Two details of the mechanism are worth having exactly, because they are what make it
        survivable rather than merely elegant.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, credits are delivered <em>in band</em> — carried in the link's own traffic rather
        than as a separate signalling channel, which is what the tutorial contrasts against
        "pause/xon-xoff schemes".<Cite ids="ieee-ib-credits" /> Second, and more importantly, they
        are <em>absolute</em>: a credit advertisement is a running total of everything permitted
        since the link initialised, not an increment. That choice is a resiliency decision. An
        incremental scheme desynchronises permanently if one update is lost; an absolute one
        self-corrects on the very next advertisement, because the next number supersedes the last.
        The named primary challenge in this design is not the accounting — it is the loss of a
        flow-control update itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Credits are issued per <T id="virtual-lane">virtual lane</T>, so the link is not a single
        credited channel but several independently buffered ones. A lane whose receive buffers are
        full ceases transmission while the other lanes continue.<Cite ids="ibta-spec" /> That is
        the mechanism underneath InfiniBand's quality of service, and it is also the first line of
        defence against the price discussed below.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Credit versus drop"
    hint="Raise the offered load past the line rate"
    caption="An illustrative model, not a performance predictor. It assumes a retransmission costs roughly twenty times a normal transfer — the order of magnitude of a timeout against a transmission — and that a collective finishes with its slowest member. The point it makes is qualitative and robust to those constants: past saturation the lossy link degrades abruptly and unpredictably, while the credited link degrades linearly and stays predictable."
  >
    <div data-credit>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img"
        aria-label="Two links compared under the same offered load: a credit-based link which stalls its sender and delivers everything, and a lossy link which overflows its receive buffer and discards packets. Below, bars comparing the resulting collective completion time.">
        <g data-scene></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Offered load</dt><dd data-out="load">100%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Credited: delivered</dt><dd data-out="cr-del">100%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Credited: sender stalled</dt><dd data-out="cr-stall">0%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Lossy: dropped</dt><dd data-out="lo-drop">0%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Collective, credited</dt><dd data-out="cr-time">1.00×</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Collective, lossy</dt><dd data-out="lo-time">1.00×</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="verdict" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="load">Offered load<output data-out="load-lbl">100%</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="load" data-load min="40" max="140" value="100" step="1" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="price">What losslessness costs</h2>

    <p>
      Nothing about this is free. A network that refuses to discard must instead refuse to accept,
      and that refusal propagates.
    </p>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        When a receiver stops returning credits, its upstream sender stops transmitting. If that
        sender is a switch, its own buffers begin to fill, and it in turn stops returning credits
        to <em>its</em> upstream. Backpressure walks the fabric in the opposite direction to the
        traffic that caused it, and it can reach senders that had nothing to do with the congested
        link. This is <T id="head-of-line">head-of-line blocking</T>: a packet that cannot move
        holds up packets queued behind it that could have.
      </p>
      <p>
        A lossy network never has this problem, because it resolves congestion by destroying the
        blocked packet. Losslessness is the decision to hold the packet instead — and to accept
        that holding it has consequences elsewhere.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        The practical upshot is that a lossless fabric needs two further mechanisms that a lossy
        one can do without, and both appear later in this section. Virtual lanes contain
        backpressure within a traffic class, so a saturated class cannot freeze the others.
        <a href="/routing#adaptive">Adaptive routing</a> moves flows off the congested path
        entirely, which is the only way to stop backpressure rather than merely bound it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is also the reason the equivalent Ethernet story is harder rather than simply
        different. <T id="roce">RoCE</T> runs the same RDMA semantics over Ethernet and therefore
        needs the same lossless guarantee, but it has to build it from
        <T id="pfc">PFC</T> and <T id="ecn">ECN</T> — a reactive pause and an end-to-end congestion
        signal — rather than getting it from the link layer by construction.
        <a href="/infiniband-vs-ethernet">Chapter 7</a> is about how close that gets.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        Hold on to the asymmetry: a lossy network converts congestion into <em>latency for the
        unlucky</em>, and a lossless one converts it into <em>backpressure for everyone</em>. When
        the workload is thousands of independent flows the first is obviously right. When the
        workload is one synchronising collective across ten thousand GPUs, the second is the only
        option that finishes.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"Lossless means congestion has been solved."</strong> It means congestion has been
        relocated. The queue that would have overflowed in the fabric now forms at the sender, and
        the fabric's job becomes keeping that queue short — which is what routing, topology and
        congestion control spend their time on. A lossless fabric under sustained overload is
        still a slow fabric; it is just a predictable one.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Deeper title="Why the specification reference here is marked unconfirmed">
      <p>
        The normative source for all of this is the InfiniBand Architecture Specification, Volume
        1, section 7.9 — and it is not openly downloadable. Access requires registration with the
        IBTA, so this site has not read it, and its source entry says so.<Cite ids="ibta-spec" />
      </p>
      <p>
        What has been read is the 2014 tutorial presented to the IEEE 802.1 Data Center Bridging
        task group, which states the mechanism publicly and cites that section
        directly.<Cite ids="ieee-ib-credits" /> Everything asserted above traces to it. This is
        the same standard the rest of the site holds itself to: a citation is only marked verified
        when the document behind it was actually opened, and
        <a href="/sources#gaps">the ones that were not are listed</a>.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-credit]');
    if (root) {
      const fig = root.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const scene = root.querySelector('[data-scene]')!;
      const slider = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-load]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };
      const text = (x: number, y: number, t: string, extra: Record<string, string | number> = {}) => {
        const e = el('text', { x, y, ...extra });
        e.textContent = t;
        return e;
      };

      /*
       * A retransmission costs far more than a transmission — a timeout rather
       * than a round trip. Twenty is the order of magnitude, not a measurement,
       * and the widget's caption says so. The conclusion does not depend on the
       * exact value: any figure above about three produces the same shape.
       */
      const RETRANSMIT_COST = 20;

      const render = () => {
        const load = Number(slider.value);
        const over = Math.max(0, load - 100);

        // Credited: nothing is ever discarded, so the excess becomes sender
        // stall and the collective simply takes proportionally longer.
        const crStall = over / load * 100;
        const crTime = Math.max(1, load / 100);

        // Lossy: the excess overflows the receive buffer and is discarded. The
        // collective waits for whoever has to ask again.
        const loDrop = over / load * 100;
        const loTime = 1 + (loDrop / 100) * RETRANSMIT_COST;

        out('load').textContent = load + '%';
        out('load-lbl').textContent = load + '%';
        out('cr-del').textContent = '100%';
        out('cr-stall').textContent = crStall.toFixed(0) + '%';
        out('lo-drop').textContent = loDrop.toFixed(1) + '%';
        out('cr-time').textContent = crTime.toFixed(2) + '×';
        out('lo-time').textContent = loTime.toFixed(2) + '×';

        out('verdict').textContent = over === 0
          ? 'Below the line rate the two links are indistinguishable — which is why a fabric can look fine in testing and fall over in production. The difference only appears once demand exceeds what the link can carry, and a collective makes every GPU demand at once.'
          : `At ${load}% offered load the credited link stalls its sender ${crStall.toFixed(0)}% of the time and still delivers everything, finishing in ${crTime.toFixed(2)}× the ideal. The lossy link discards ${loDrop.toFixed(1)}% and finishes in ${loTime.toFixed(2)}×, because the collective waits for whoever has to ask again.`;

        // ── Draw ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        scene.textContent = '';
        const lanes = [
          { y: 30, label: 'Credit-based', accent: 'var(--cyan)', stalled: crStall, dropped: 0 },
          { y: 130, label: 'Drop on overflow', accent: 'var(--danger)', stalled: 0, dropped: loDrop },
        ];

        for (const lane of lanes) {
          scene.appendChild(text(0, lane.y - 8, lane.label, { fill: lane.accent }));
          // sender
          scene.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 0, y: lane.y, width: 78, height: 34, rx: 4,
            fill: 'none', stroke: 'var(--line-strong)' }));
          scene.appendChild(text(39, lane.y + 21, 'sender', { 'text-anchor': 'middle' }));
          // link
          scene.appendChild(el('line', { x1: 78, y1: lane.y + 17, x2: 560, y2: lane.y + 17,
            stroke: 'var(--line-strong)', 'stroke-width': 1 }));
          // packets in flight, thinned by whatever the lane loses or stalls
          const inFlight = Math.round(14 * (1 - (lane.stalled + lane.dropped) / 100));
          for (let i = 0; i < inFlight; i++) {
            scene.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 92 + i * 32, y: lane.y + 11, width: 20, height: 12,
              rx: 2, fill: lane.accent, opacity: 0.75 }));
          }
          // receive buffer
          scene.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 560, y: lane.y - 4, width: 96, height: 42, rx: 4,
            fill: 'var(--panel-2)', stroke: 'var(--line-strong)' }));
          scene.appendChild(text(608, lane.y + 21, 'buffer', { 'text-anchor': 'middle' }));

          if (lane.stalled > 0) {
            scene.appendChild(text(39, lane.y + 50, `stalled ${lane.stalled.toFixed(0)}%`,
              { 'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: lane.accent }));
          }
          if (lane.dropped > 0) {
            // Discarded packets, drawn falling out of the full buffer.
            const n = Math.max(1, Math.round(lane.dropped / 4));
            for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
              scene.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 566 + i * 10, y: lane.y + 46, width: 7, height: 7,
                rx: 1, fill: 'var(--danger)', opacity: 0.8 }));
            }
            scene.appendChild(text(716, lane.y + 56, `${lane.dropped.toFixed(1)}% dropped`,
              { fill: 'var(--danger)', 'text-anchor': 'end' }));
          }
        }

        // ── Completion bars ───────────────────────────────────────────
        const worst = Math.max(crTime, loTime, 1.2);
        scene.appendChild(text(0, 236, 'Collective completion, multiples of ideal'));
        const bars = [
          { y: 246, t: crTime, fill: 'var(--cyan)', name: 'credited' },
          { y: 272, t: loTime, fill: 'var(--danger)', name: 'lossy' },
        ];
        for (const b of bars) {
          scene.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 76, y: b.y, width: Math.max(2, (b.t / worst) * 520),
            height: 16, rx: 3, fill: b.fill, opacity: 0.85 }));
          scene.appendChild(text(70, b.y + 12, b.name, { 'text-anchor': 'end' }));
          scene.appendChild(text(82 + (b.t / worst) * 520, b.y + 12, b.t.toFixed(2) + '×'));
        }
      };

      slider.addEventListener('input', render);
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
